From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 07:30:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Revert "Upgrade tinyxml2 to 11.0.0"

11.0.0 changed DynArray and MemPoolT to hold their sizes as size_t
instead of int:

  -template <class T, int INITIAL_SIZE>
  +template <class T, size_t INITIAL_SIZE>
  -    int Size() const
  +    size_t Size() const

That grows every embedded DynArray by 8 bytes, and XMLDocument embeds
several of them, so sizeof(XMLDocument) grows with it.

Prebuilt camera HALs stack-allocate an XMLDocument. bonito's
camera.sdm710.so is one: CamX::ImageSensorUtils::ReadSensorCalibration()
parses camera_imu_average_calibration.xml into an XMLDocument on its
stack, with the space reserved when the blob was compiled against a
tinyxml2 whose XMLDocument was smaller. With 11.0.0 the constructor
writes past that reservation and over the register save area --
confirmed with a watchpoint on the saved slot, which trapped the write
inside /vendor/lib64/libtinyxml2.so.

The register it lands on is x20, which
ImageSensorModuleData::GetStaticCaps() is holding its TuningDataManager
in. The epilogue restores it as NULL, GetStaticCaps() then calls
GetTunedColorCorrectionData(nullptr, ...), and TuningDataManager::
GetChromatix() dereferences it. The camera provider SIGSEGVs before
registering, so the device enumerates zero cameras.

Nothing in the logs shows this: every CamX message is identical to a
working build, because no CamX state is wrong -- a register is trampled
underneath it.

Confirmed by bind-mounting 10.0.0's libtinyxml2.so over the 11.0.0 one
on a running 24.0 device: the provider survives and cameraserver
enumerates 2 cameras. Revert until the vendor copy can be pinned
separately from the platform one.

